   "id": "us-rate-fear-slide",
   "group": "United States",
   "headline": "US stocks slide for a sixth day as a larger Fed rate rise looks possible",
   "when": "Thu 19 Nov · US close",
   "what": "The S&P 500 fell 2.02% to 7,175.83 and the Nasdaq fell 2.25% to 23,879.20. The S&P 500 has now dropped six sessions in a row. The 10-year Treasury yield reached 5.11% during the day and ended near 5.05%. Traders put about a 94% chance on a quarter-point Fed rise in December and 6% on a half-point. Minutes of the last Fed meeting showed some officials open to a move larger than a quarter point, and Governor Waller said he favours a half point.",
   "why": "About 17% of your portfolio sits in US large-cap funds, mostly the S&P 500 feeder. Both US funds are priced at 17 Nov, when the S&P 500 stood about 2.2% higher, so this morning's dashboard does not yet show the fall. It should show up when newer NAVs arrive at the 11:30 refresh. Higher yields also weigh on your single US stocks.",
